1
00:00:00,720 --> 00:00:03,359
>> Long live Animal Farm.

2
00:00:03,359 --> 00:00:09,840
>> And after the singing of Beasts of England, the animals were dismissed.

3
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:15,640
Afterwards, Squealer made a round of the farm and set the animals' minds at rest.

4
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:19,720
He assured them that the resolution against engaging in trade and using

5
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:24,199
money had never been passed or even suggested.

6
00:00:24,199 --> 00:00:31,520
It was pure imagination, probably traceable in the beginning to lies circulated by Snowball.

7
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:37,439
A few animals still felt faintly doubtful, but Squealer asked them shrewdly,

8
00:00:37,439 --> 00:00:40,319
>> "Are you certain that this is not something that you have dreamed,

9
00:00:40,319 --> 00:00:46,399
comrades? Have you any record of such a resolution? Is it written down anywhere?

10
00:00:46,399 --> 00:00:50,680
>> And since it was certainly true that nothing of the kind existed in writing,

11
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:54,479
the animals were satisfied that they had been mistaken.

12
00:00:54,479 --> 00:00:59,079
Every Monday, Mr. Whimper visited the farm as had been arranged. He was a

13
00:00:59,079 --> 00:01:05,319
sly-looking little man with side whiskers, a solicitor in a very small way of business,

14
00:01:05,319 --> 00:01:09,279
but sharp enough to have realized earlier than anyone else that Animal

15
00:01:09,279 --> 00:01:14,279
Farm would need a broker and that the commissions would be worth having.

16
00:01:14,279 --> 00:01:19,239
The animals watched his coming and going with a kind of dread and avoided him as

17
00:01:19,239 --> 00:01:21,039
much as possible.

18
00:01:21,039 --> 00:01:26,920
Nevertheless, the sight of Napoleon on all fours delivering orders to Whimper

19
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:33,720
who stood on two legs roused their pride and partly reconciled them to the new arrangement.

20
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:39,640
Their relations with the human race were now not quite the same as they had been before.

21
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:43,840
The human beings did not hate Animal Farm any less now that it was

22
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:48,399
prospering. Indeed, they hated it more than ever.

23
00:01:48,399 --> 00:01:54,000
Every human being held it as an article of faith that the farm would go bankrupt

24
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,359
sooner or later and, above all, that the windmill would be a failure.

25
00:02:00,359 --> 00:02:04,600
They would meet in the public houses and prove to one another by means of

26
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:11,560
diagrams that the windmill was bound to fall down or that if it did stand up,

27
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:14,439
then that it would never work.

28
00:02:14,439 --> 00:02:19,319
And yet, against their will, they had developed a certain respect for the

29
00:02:19,319 --> 00:02:23,720
efficiency with which the animals were managing their own affairs.

30
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:28,399
One symptom of this was that they had begun to call Animal Farm by its proper

31
00:02:28,399 --> 00:02:34,079
name and ceased to pretend that it was called the Manor Farm.

32
00:02:34,079 --> 00:02:38,319
They had also dropped their championship of Jones who had given up hope of

33
00:02:38,319 --> 00:02:43,000
getting his farm back and gone to live in another part of the county.

34
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,600
Except through Whymper, there was as yet no contact between Animal Farm and the

35
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:53,520
outside world. But there were constant rumors that Napoleon was about to enter

36
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:59,119
into a definite business agreement either with Mr. Pilkington of Foxwood or

37
00:02:59,119 --> 00:03:02,039
with Mr. Frederick of Pinchfield.

38
00:03:02,039 --> 00:03:07,199
But never, it was noticed, with both simultaneously.

39
00:03:07,199 --> 00:03:11,479
It was about this time that the pigs suddenly moved into the farmhouse and

40
00:03:11,479 --> 00:03:13,800
took up their residence there.

41
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:18,520
Again, the animals seemed to remember that a resolution against this had been

42
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:23,640
passed in the early days. And again, Squealer was able to convince them that

43
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:26,199
this was not the case.

44
00:03:26,199 --> 00:03:31,840
"It was absolutely necessary," he said, "that the pigs, who were the brains of

45
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:35,920
the farm, should have a quiet place to work in.

46
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:41,880
It was also more suited to the dignity of the leader, for of late he had taken

47
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:45,960
to speaking of Napoleon under the title of leader,

48
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:50,079
to live in a house than in a mere sty."

49
00:03:50,079 --> 00:03:54,600
Nevertheless, some of the animals were disturbed when they heard that the pigs

50
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:58,920
not only took their meals in the kitchen and used the drawing room as a

51
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:03,920
recreation room, but also slept in the beds.

52
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:09,960
Boxer passed it off as usual with >> "Napoleon is always right."

53
00:04:09,960 --> 00:04:15,000
>> But Clover, who thought she remembered a definite ruling against beds,

54
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
went to the end of the barn and tried to puzzle out the Seven Commandments, which

55
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,520
were inscribed there.

56
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:28,560
Finding herself unable to read more than individual letters, she fetched Muriel.

57
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:32,199
>> "Muriel, read me the Fourth Commandment.

58
00:04:32,199 --> 00:04:37,439
Does it not say something about never sleeping in a bed.

59
00:04:38,479 --> 00:04:41,760
>> With some difficulty, Muriel spelt it out.

60
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:47,079
>> It says, "No animal shall sleep in a bed with sheets."

61
00:04:47,079 --> 00:04:53,439
>> Curiously enough, Clover had not remembered that the fourth commandment mentioned sheets.

62
00:04:53,439 --> 00:04:58,319
But as it was there on the wall, it must have done so. And Squealer, who happened

63
00:04:58,319 --> 00:05:03,279
to be passing at this moment, attended by two or three dogs, was able to put

64
00:05:03,279 --> 00:05:06,760
the whole matter in its proper perspective.

65
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:10,640
>> "You have heard then, comrades, that we pigs now sleep in the beds of the

66
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:15,680
farmhouse. And why not? You did not suppose, surely, that there was ever a

67
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:21,479
ruling against beds. A bed merely means a place to sleep in. A pile of straw in

68
00:05:21,479 --> 00:05:26,560
a stall is a bed, properly regarded. The rule was against sheets, which are a

69
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:31,600
human invention. We have removed the sheets from the farmhouse beds and sleep

70
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:36,560
between blankets. And very comfortable beds they are, too. But not more

71
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:40,680
comfortable than we need, I can tell you, comrades, with all the brain work

72
00:05:40,680 --> 00:05:44,800
we have to do nowadays. You would not rob us of our repose, would you,

73
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:50,079
comrades? You would not have us too tired to carry out our duties. Surely,

74
00:05:50,079 --> 00:05:53,119
none of you wishes to see Jones back."

75
00:05:53,119 --> 00:05:57,399
>> The animals reassured him on this point immediately, and no more was said about

76
00:05:57,399 --> 00:06:00,119
the pigs sleeping in the farmhouse beds.

77
00:06:00,119 --> 00:06:03,000
And when, some days afterwards, it was announced that from now on the pigs
